  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What anonymous actually means</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Removing names does not make a dataset anonymous, and the word is often used for data that is not. The UK Information Commissioner’s Office notes that datasets are commonly described as anonymised when they still hold personal data in pseudonymised form, and that anonymising personal data is itself a form of processing.</p>
      <p class="reveal">The test is whether re-identification is reasonably likely, not whether it is merely conceivable. Rare events, small groups and the surrounding context can identify people after the names are gone, which is why personal information gets its own review, separate from rights, and why a scope can be narrowed or declined.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What we hold, and what we do not</h2>
      <p class="reveal">We do not take custody of your records. Nothing is copied to us, and nothing is delivered to a buyer until you have approved the scope in writing. The agreement is between your company and the buyer you accept, and that buyer pays you directly.</p>
      <p class="reveal">Questions about that agreement, your data rights or how the data is used go to the buyer, and your own counsel can review the terms before you sign.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What happens to a copy once it is delivered</h2>
      <p class="reveal">The program we introduce companies to publishes the terms it works to, and they are worth reading directly rather than relying on any summary of them, including this one.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Processing before use</strong>About 15 to 30 days of processing follows delivery, on the program&rsquo;s published terms.</li>
        <li><strong>Deletion of the raw upload</strong>The published term is deletion within 120 days of delivery. De-identified material can outlive that copy, which is why the transformation matters as much as the deletion date.</li>
        <li><strong>An audit right</strong>The published terms allow a sample of the de-identified copy to be inspected before it is sent.</li>
        <li><strong>A deletion date in writing</strong>The end of the term is set in the agreement you sign, not in a conversation.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Questions we put to a buyer before an introduction</h2>
      <p class="reveal">An introduction is worth making only if the buyer can answer these plainly. The same list works if a buyer approaches you directly.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>What exactly is licensed, and for how long</strong>Scope and term, with exclusivity stated rather than implied.</li>
        <li><strong>Where the copy lives, and for how long</strong>Storage, who can access it, and the deletion date for the raw upload.</li>
        <li><strong>What happens at the end of the term</strong>Whether the licence renews automatically, must be renegotiated, or ends.</li>
        <li><strong>Who can see it inside the buyer</strong>Not only the buyer company, but the AI lab that would use it.</li>
        <li><strong>What triggers payment</strong>Acceptance, invoice and delivery are three different dates.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal">The <a href="ai-buyers.html">questions to ask any buyer</a> page lists the rest, and <a href="buyer-terms.html">buyer terms side by side</a> shows which of them each program publishes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

